From 3feab54e05af22b328aa98be6b07ca91711c8a87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Burke Cates <burke.cates@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 08:34:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] NOT-FOR-UPSTREAM: soundwire: qcom: check the bus on system resume, before
 streams restart

When the controller has users at system suspend it is forced into clock
stop, and pm_runtime_force_resume() brings it out again during system
resume. On the ASUS Zenbook A16 (Glymur, two WSA8845 on each of two
buses) that clock-stop exit faulted on 25-45 % of s2idle resumes: a bus
clash, parity errors on both amplifiers, MAX_RETRY on alert reads,
occasionally AUTO_ENUM_FAILED. The clash handler only queues the
recovery worker, which then raced the rest of resume, and user space
resumed the stream into the broken bus.

User space is still frozen while the system resume callbacks run, and a
stream stopped for system suspend (its SoundWire stream disabled by the
machine driver) is only restarted by it. So the resume callback is the
place to repair the bus: let the clock-stop exit settle (up to 100 ms
for the peripherals to report attached), wait for the interrupt thread,
and if a fault was reported or a peripheral is missing, reset and
re-enumerate synchronously. A disabled stream is re-programmed by the
next sdw_prepare_stream().

Peripherals that never bound are not reprobed from here: probing is
blocked until the system transition completes. Anything still pending
is left to the worker.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (claude-opus-5-5)
---
Not-for-upstream: local workaround for one laptop, not reviewed for
  submission; use only for local testing.

 drivers/soundwire/qcom.c | 31 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
index fe581ab0156d..ad9100d5dba8 100644
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -147,6 +147,7 @@
 #define SWR_OVERFLOW_RETRY_COUNT				30
 #define SWRM_LINK_STATUS_RETRY_CNT				100
 #define SWRM_IRQ_MAX_LOOPS					32
+#define SWRM_RESUME_SETTLE_MS					100
 
 enum {
 	MASTER_ID_WSA = 1,
@@ -2357,11 +2358,39 @@ static int __maybe_unused swrm_system_resume(struct device *dev)
 
 	ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
 
+	mutex_lock(&qcom_swrm_probe_recovery_lock);
 	mutex_lock(&ctrl->recovery_lock);
 	ctrl->system_suspended = false;
+
+	/*
+	 * If the controller came out of clock stop here (it had users at
+	 * suspend), check the bus before returning. Streams stopped for
+	 * system suspend are only restarted by user space, which is still
+	 * frozen, so this is the window in which a fault from the clock-stop
+	 * exit (a clash, parity errors, a peripheral that did not come back)
+	 * can be repaired before any port is enabled or amplifier unmuted.
+	 */
+	if (!ret && pm_runtime_active(dev) && READ_ONCE(ctrl->recovery_ready) &&
+	    !qcom_swrm_stream_running(ctrl)) {
+		wait_event_timeout(ctrl->slaves_wait,
+				   qcom_swrm_all_slaves_online(ctrl),
+				   msecs_to_jiffies(SWRM_RESUME_SETTLE_MS));
+		synchronize_irq(ctrl->irq);
+		if (!qcom_swrm_all_slaves_online(ctrl) &&
+		    !atomic_read(&ctrl->recovery_pending)) {
+			dev_warn(dev, "peripherals not attached after resume\n");
+			atomic_inc(&ctrl->recovery_pending);
+		}
+		if (atomic_read(&ctrl->recovery_pending)) {
+			dev_info(dev, "recovering the bus on system resume\n");
+			qcom_swrm_recover(ctrl, false);
+		}
+	}
+
 	mutex_unlock(&ctrl->recovery_lock);
+	mutex_unlock(&qcom_swrm_probe_recovery_lock);
 
-	/* A fault reported while suspended or during resume. */
+	/* Whatever is still pending runs once streams allow it. */
 	if (atomic_read(&ctrl->recovery_pending))
 		schedule_work(&ctrl->recovery_work);
 
